are as of the previous quarter-end.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [842, 1711, 1486, 1794], \"page_id\": 72}], \"content\": \"4 As of January 2008, includes Austria, Belgium, Cyprus, Finland, France, Germany, Greece, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, Malta, the Netherlands, Portugal, Slovenia, and Spain. For data as of January 2009, also includes Slovakia. For data as of January 2011, also includes Estonia.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [842, 1791, 1483, 1824], \"page_id\": 72}], \"content\": \"Includes European, Latin American, Caribbean, Asian, African, and Middle East regional organizations.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1337, 1994, 1486, 2013], \"page_id\": 72}], \"content\": \"December 2011\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"page_footer\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 87, 184, 127], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"68\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [658, 87, 974, 132], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"CAPITAL MOVEMENTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [153, 182, 1481, 297], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"CHART CM-A.\\u2014U.S. Liabilities to Foreigners Reported by U.S. Banks, Brokers, and Dealers with Respect to Selected Countries\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [255, 328, 1366, 874], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [284, 943, 1344, 1487], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [217, 1518, 1426, 1721], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"U.S. liabilities to foreigners reported by U.S. banks were recorded at $4.8 trillion in September 2011, an increase of $205 billion from yearend 2010. U.S. banking liabilities include foreign holdings of U.S. short-term securities but exclude foreign holdings of U.S. long-term securities. U.S. banking liabilities decreased about $427 billion in 2009 but increased about $200 billion in 2008 and about $160 billion in 2010. However, the 2008 increase is primarily attributable to the addition of several new bank holding companies to the reporter panel.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [224, 1728, 1426, 1959], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"U.S. banking liabilities are concentrated in international financial centers. The data on this page show that over 60 percent of U.S. banking liabilities is currently recorded against the United Kingdom and banking centers in the Caribbean. These financial centers recorded most of the decline in banking liabilities in 2008 and 2009. However, the crisis affected other regions as well, including all other Europe and Asia (including Japan), all of which experienced a decline in banking liabilities against the U.S. in 2009 and 2010. In contrast, during the third quarter of 2011, U.S. liabilities to foreigners grew for most regions. The Caribbean and all other Asia were the exceptions, with U.S. liabilities falling slightly over those recorded in 2010.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 1985, 299, 2022], \"page_id\": 73}], \"content\": \"December 2011\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"page_footer\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [665, 99, 967, 125], \"page_id\": 74}], \"content\": \"CAPITAL MOVEMENTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1454, 97, 1494, 127], \"page_id\": 74}], \"content\": \"69\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [191, 198, 1435, 240], \"page_id\": 74}], \"content\": \"SECTION II.\\u2014Claims on Foreigners Reported