": 82, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 83, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 84, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 85, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 86, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 87, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 88, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 89, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 90, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 91, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 92, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 93, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 94, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 95, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 96, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 97, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 98, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 99, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 100, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 101, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 102, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 103, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 104, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 105, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 106, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 107, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 108, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 109, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 110, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 111, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 112, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 113, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 114, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 115, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 116, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 117, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 118, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 119, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 120, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 121, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 122, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 123, \"image_uri\": null}, {\"id\": 124, \"image_uri\": null}]}, \"error_status\": null, \"metadata\": {}}\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:Refunds of collections generally are treated as reductions of collections, whereas payments for earned-income tax credits in excess of tax liabilities are treated as outlays. Outlays during a fiscal year may be for payment of obligations incurred in prior years or in the same year. Outlays, therefore, flow in part from unexpended balances of prior year budget authority and from budget authority provided for the year in which the money is spent. Total outlays include both budget and off-budget outlays and are stated net of offsetting collections.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:Offsetting receipts are generally deducted from budget authority and outlays by function, subfunction, or agency. There are four types of receipts, however, that are deducted from budget totals as undistributed offsetting receipts. They are: (1) agencies' payments (including payments by off-budget Federal entities) as employers into employees' retirement funds; (2) interest received by trust funds; (3) rents and royalties on the Outer Continental Shelf lands; and (4) other interest (i.e., that collected on Outer Continental Shelf money in deposit funds when such money is transferred into the budget).\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:The Government has used the unified budget concept set forth in the “Report of the President’s Commission on Budget Concepts” as a foundation for its budgetary analysis and presentation since 1969. The concept calls for the budget to include all of the Government’s fiscal transactions with the public. Since 1971, however, various laws have been enacted removing several Federal entities from (or creating them outside of) the budget. Other laws have moved certain off-budget Federal entities onto the budget. Under current law, the off-budget Federal entities consist of the two Social Security trust funds, Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance and the Federal Disability Insurance Trust Fund, and the Postal Service.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:Although an off-budget Federal entity's receipts, outlays, and surplus or deficit ordinarily are not subject to targets set by the Congressional resolution, the Balanced Budget and Emergency Deficit Control Act of 1985 [commonly known as the Gramm-Rudman-Hollings Act as amended by the Budget Enforcement Act of 1990 (2 United States Code 900-922)] included off-budget surplus or deficit in calculating deficit targets under that act and in calculating excess deficit. Partly for this reason, attention has focused on both on- and off-budget receipts, outlays and deficit of the Government.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:Tables FFO-1, FFO-2, and FFO-3 are published quarterly and cover 5 years of data, estimates for 2 years, detail for 13 months, and fiscal year-to-date data. They provide a summary of data relating to Federal fiscal operations reported by Federal entities and disbursing officers, and daily reports from the FRBs. They also detail accounting transactions affecting receipts and outlays of the Government and off-budget Federal entities and their related effect on assets and liabilities of the Government. Data are derived from the \"Monthly Treasury Statement of Receipts and Outlays of the United States Government.\"\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:Table FFO-2 includes on- and off-budget receipts by source. Amounts represent income taxes, social insurance taxes, net contributions for other insurance and retirement, excise taxes, estate and gift taxes, customs duties, and net miscellaneous receipts.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:Table FFO-3 details on- and off-budget outlays by agency.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:Table FFO-4 summarizes on- and off-budget receipts by source and outlays by function as reported to each major fund group classification for the current fiscal year to date and prior fiscal year to date.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:| Total on- and off-budget results: | nan | nan |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:| Total on- and off-budget financing | 328088.0 | 1298613.0 |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:| Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results | Total on-budget and off-budget results |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:| Employment and general retirement (off-budget) | - | - | 565787 | 565787.0 | - | - | 631688 | 631688.0 |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:| Employment and general retirement (on-budget) | - | - | 192728 | 192728.0 | - | - | 184208 | 184208.0 |\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2011_12.txt:Intrabudgetary transactions (“Federal Fiscal Operations”)—These occur when payment and receipt both occur within the budget, or when payment is made from off-budget Federal entities whose budget authority and outlays are excluded from the budget totals.\n/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_2014_12.json:{\"document\": {\"elements\": [{\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [905, 160, 1213, 1049], \"page_id\": 1}], \"content\": \"TREASURY BULLETIN DECEMBER 2014\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [942, 1499, 1213, 1560], \"page_id\": 1}], \"content\": \"FEATURES\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [945, 1570, 1213, 1619], \"page_id\": 1}], \"content\": \"Profile of the Economy\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [973, 1629, 1213, 1678], \"page_id\": 1}], \"content\": \"Financial Operations\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [942, 1688, 1213, 1732], \"page_id\": 1}], \"content\": \"International Statistics\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1027, 1742, 1213, 1789], \"page_id\": 1}], \"content\": \"Special Reports\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1011, 1836, 1213, 1867], \"page_id\": 1}], \"content\": \"Produced and Published by\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [860, 1890, 1219, 1945], \"page_id\": 1}], \"content\": \"Department of the Treasury Bureau of the Fiscal Service\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [204, 264, 1428, 1850], \"page_id\": 2}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [182, 780, 1450, 971], \"page_id\": 3}], \"content\": \"The Treasury Bulletin is issued quarterly in March, June, September, and December by the Bureau of the Fiscal Service, Governmentwide Accounting, Budget Reports Division. Statistical data is compiled from sources within Treasury departmental offices and bureaus, as well as various other Federal program agencies. Readers can contact the publication staff at (202) 874-9939 or (304) 480-5162 to inquire about any of the published information. Suggestions are welcome.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [415, 992, 1217, 1037], \"page_id\": 3}], \"content\": \"The publication staff can also be reached by electronic mail.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [519, 1077, 1093, 1117], \"page_id\": 3}], \"content\": \"treasury.bulletin@fiscal.treasury.gov\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [204, 1138, 1428, 1223], \"page_id\": 3}], \"content\": \"Internet service subscribers can access the Treasury Bulletin in Microsoft Word or PDF format through the Bureau of the Fiscal Service's home page.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [625, 1268, 984, 1308], \"page_id\": 3}], \"content\": \"www.fiscal.treasury.gov\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [565, 191, 1066, 259], \"page_id\": 4}], \"content\": \"Table of Contents\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"typ